---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="BLZ &amp; the plaintext-ARM9 trick"
  description="Backward-LZ compression Nintendo uses for executable ARM9 code, plus the CompressedStaticEnd patch that lets us load ARM9 as plaintext for in-place string editing."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'BLZ + plaintext-ARM9 trick' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Nintendo's DS firmware boots an ARM9 binary that's compressed with a
    custom backward-LZ scheme commonly called <strong>BLZ</strong> ("Backwards
    LZ"). Unlike the
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/lz11/`}>LZ11</a> wrapper used
    for asset containers, BLZ is designed for in-place decompression of
    executable code: the compressed bytes occupy the high end of a buffer,
    and the decompressor walks backward through them, writing literal bytes
    and back-references into the same buffer toward lower addresses. The
    decompressed code ends up at the buffer base, and the BLZ footer at the
    high end is overwritten by the last few decompressed bytes.
  </p>

  <h2>Where BLZ is used in this ROM</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>ROM offset</th><th>Compressed size</th><th>Decompressed size</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Main ARM9 binary</td>
        <td><code>0x4000</code></td>
        <td>564,488 bytes</td>
        <td>723,032 bytes (<code>0xB0858</code>)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Overlay <code>overlay_NNNN.bin</code> (most)</td>
        <td>per overlay table at <code>0x8DE00</code></td>
        <td>varies (see overlay table)</td>
        <td>varies; runtime decompressed by engine</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Overlays use the same code-LZ variant but with a per-file
    <code>is_compressed</code> flag in the overlay table. Flip that bit to
    <code>0</code> and store the overlay body uncompressed and the engine
    will load it as plaintext — that's the same idea as the ARM9
    <code>CompressedStaticEnd</code> trick, applied per overlay.
  </p>

  <h2>BLZ footer</h2>
  <p>
    The last 8 bytes of a BLZ-compressed payload form the footer the
    backward decompressor reads first:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Offset (from end)</th><th>Size</th><th>Field</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>-0x08</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>add_size</code> — bytes to grow the buffer past the compressed region (= decompressed length − compressed length).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-0x04</code></td><td>3</td><td><code>header_size</code> — bytes from the buffer end that are footer + literal-tail (decompressor stops after consuming these).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-0x01</code></td><td>1</td><td>Reserved / pad.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Decompressed-payload layout (the "module params" struct)</h2>
  <p>
    Once the BLZ payload is decompressed, the resulting ARM9 plaintext starts
    with a small NitroSDK <strong>module params</strong> struct that the
    secure-boot loader uses to set up <code>.bss</code> initialization and
    decide whether to skip the compression step on subsequent loads. The
    fields most relevant to patching are at the very start of the plaintext:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Plaintext offset</th><th>Field</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>+0x14</code></td><td><code>AutoLoadDoneCallback</code></td><td>Set after <code>.bss</code> init runs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x1C</code></td><td><code>CompressedStaticEnd</code></td><td>Pointer to the end of the BLZ-compressed region in main RAM. <strong>If this equals <code>StaticBSSStart</code></strong>, the loader treats the binary as already-decompressed and skips BLZ entirely.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x20</code></td><td><code>StaticBSSStart</code></td><td>Pointer to the start of <code>.bss</code> (zero-initialized region).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x24</code></td><td><code>StaticBSSEnd</code></td><td>Pointer to the end of <code>.bss</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The "plaintext ARM9" patcher trick</h2>
  <p>
    For string-pool edits like the 26-slot ARM9 menu pool (see
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/arm9-menu-pool/`}>ARM9 menu pool</a>),
    re-running the BLZ encoder on every patch attempt is awkward — the
    encoder is fiddly, the compressed-size footprint shifts as strings
    change, and round-trip verification is slow. Easier: load the ARM9 as
    plaintext and skip BLZ entirely.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Patch the module-params field <code>CompressedStaticEnd := StaticBSSStart</code> in the ARM9 plaintext (in this ROM, <code>StaticBSSStart = 0x020A87A0</code>).</li>
    <li>Store the entire decompressed ARM9 (723,032 bytes) verbatim at ROM offset <code>0x4000</code> in place of the original 564,488-byte BLZ blob.</li>
    <li>Update the NDS header's ARM9 size field to <code>0xB0858</code> (the decompressed length).</li>
    <li>Boot. The loader sees <code>CompressedStaticEnd == StaticBSSStart</code>, decides "nothing to decompress," and copies the plaintext bytes verbatim to main RAM.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    From that point on, editing the embedded string pool is just bytes-at-an-offset:
    locate the slot, write the UTF-16-LE EN string with NUL pad, done. The
    Nintendo SDK validation marker
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlay-knowledgebase/`}>NitroCodeBE</a>
    isn't present in this ROM (Konami stripped it), so there's no extra
    fix-up needed for that.
  </p>

  <h2>Re-compression research</h2>
  <p>
    The plaintext-ARM9 trick is the simple path. For overlays we keep an
    alternative path open: re-LZ-compress the patched overlay binary using
    <code>ndspy.codeCompression.compress()</code> and ship the re-compressed
    blob with the original <code>is_compressed</code> bit still set. v2.32
    ships overlay patches this way (re-compressed, matching the Konami
    on-disk shape closer than plaintext-overlay would). The per-overlay
    fit/no-fit analysis lives at
    <code>notes/_lz_compression_research.py</code> in the translation repo —
    it round-trips each of the six v2.x-patched overlays through
    ndspy.codeCompression, simulates the worst-case (slot filled with
    placeholder x's) and the real EN translation, and reports whether the
    re-compressed size fits inside the original on-disk slot. All six
    overlays fit comfortably under the EN translation.
  </p>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>notes/arm9_passthrough_test_RESULTS.md</code> — validation of the plaintext-ARM9 trick.</li>
    <li><code>notes/_lz_compression_research.py</code> — overlay BLZ re-compression feasibility report (ndspy.codeCompression).</li>
    <li><code>src/translator/_patch_arm9_overlays.py</code> — the patcher that uses the trick.</li>
    <li><code>arm9_full_plaintext.bin</code> — the decompressed binary the patcher edits.</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
